<!-- ══════════════════ WHY THIS MATTERS ══════════════════ -->
<div class="tp" id="panel-why">
  <div class="card">
    <div class="why-section">
      <h3>The Central Problem</h3>
      <p>Every assessment is a theory of knowledge. When we choose what questions to ask, which sources to include, and whose perspectives count as evidence, we are making claims about what history is — and who belongs in it.</p>
      <div class="pull-quote">"The assessment is not neutral. Neither is the curriculum. Neither is the question of who gets to answer."</div>
      <p>Most history assessments in US schools were designed around a particular assumed student: someone whose background, language, and frame of reference aligns with the dominant narrative of American history. Students whose families, communities, and ancestors were excluded from that narrative — as enslaved people, as colonized peoples, as women, as workers — are asked to master a story in which they are objects rather than agents.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="why-section">
      <h3>What "Neutrality" Actually Does</h3>
      <p>A "neutral" assessment is one that appears to ask purely factual or analytical questions while embedding assumptions about which facts matter and which analytical frameworks are valid. When we ask "How did the New Deal respond to the Depression?" without asking "Who did the New Deal exclude?", we have made a political choice while appearing to make a technical one.</p>
      <p>This tool helps you see those choices — and make them deliberately.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="why-section">
      <h3>The Four Key Dimensions</h3>
      <div class="guide-section" style="margin-bottom:0;">
        <table class="guide-table">
          <tr><th>Dimension</th><th>What It Reveals</th><th>Key Question</th></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Perspective Gaze</strong></td><td>Whose experience is centered in sources and questions</td><td>Who is the subject of this history vs. an object in it?</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Access Load</strong></td><td>Linguistic and cultural barriers embedded in the assessment</td><td>What prior knowledge or language does this assume students bring?</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Rigor Support</strong></td><td>Whether the assessment enables high-level thinking for all students</td><td>Does this question have a high ceiling AND a low floor?</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Triangulation</strong></td><td>Whether multiple perspectives are required to answer well</td><td>Could a student answer this using only the dominant narrative?</td></tr>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="why-section">
      <h3>A Note on the Sources</h3>
      <p>All 171 sources in this library come directly from actual DREAM Charter School curriculum documents — your DBQs, SAQs, LEQs, Regents assessments, and lesson materials across APUSH, AP World, Global History, and grades 6–8. Every equity note is grounded in the specific assessments your students are taking.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ══════════════════ GUIDE ══════════════════ -->
<div class="tp" id="panel-guide">
  <div class="card">
    <div class="card-title">📐 Scoring Guide & Rubric</div>
    <div class="guide-section">
      <h3>Perspective Gaze (1–5)</h3>
      <table class="guide-table">
        <tr><th>Score</th><th>Description</th></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>5</strong></td><td>Source centers the experience of marginalized, colonized, or excluded groups as agents with their own analysis</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>4</strong></td><td>Source includes voices of marginalized groups, even if filtered through other frames</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>3</strong></td><td>Source reflects mixed perspectives; no single group dominates</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>2</strong></td><td>Source centers elite or institutional perspective but acknowledges others exist</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>1</strong></td><td>Source exclusively reflects dominant/state/elite perspective with no acknowledgment of alternatives</td></tr>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div class="guide-section">
      <h3>Access Load (1–5)</h3>
      <table class="guide-table">
        <tr><th>Score</th><th>Description</th></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>1</strong></td><td>Simple, accessible language; minimal background knowledge required; any student can engage</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>2</strong></td><td>Some specialized vocabulary; basic content knowledge helpful but not required</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>3</strong></td><td>Moderate complexity; benefits from prior familiarity with context</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>4</strong></td><td>Dense language or specialized knowledge assumed; may exclude students without specific background</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>5</strong></td><td>Highly technical, legalistic, or culturally specific; requires extensive prior knowledge</td></tr>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div class="guide-section">
      <h3>Rigor Support (1–5)</h3>
      <table class="guide-table">
        <tr><th>Score</th><th>Description</th></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>5</strong></td><td>Source enables multiple entry points and analytical approaches; rewards sophisticated thinking; high floor AND high ceiling</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>4</strong></td><td>Strong analytical potential; supports argumentation and sourcing; some scaffolding embedded</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>3</strong></td><td>Supports analysis but primarily useful for content recall or one type of historical thinking</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>2</strong></td><td>Mostly useful for fact retrieval; limited analytical potential</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>1</strong></td><td>Primarily tests recall; limited analytical entry points</td></tr>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div class="guide-section">
      <h3>Triangulation Score (Source Set)</h3>
      <table class="guide-table">
        <tr><th>Score</th><th>Description</th></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>90–100</strong></td><td>Excellent triangulation: 3+ perspectives, multiple eras, multiple source types, identity diversity</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>70–89</strong></td><td>Good triangulation: most dimensions covered; minor gaps</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>50–69</strong></td><td>Moderate: some triangulation but dominated by one perspective or type</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Below 50</strong></td><td>Poor triangulation: single perspective, limited source diversity</td></tr>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

